  <path fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd" d="M50.2471 5.37598H49.0723L49.0537 5.15332C48.9828 4.30275 48.9128 3.58296 48.6738 2.81445V2.8125C48.4002 1.91115 47.6711 1.30176 46.709 1.30176H46.707L41.3896 1.26758V8.18555H44.8486C45.1731 8.18555 45.4382 8.09314 45.6387 7.9375C45.8393 7.78168 45.9896 7.55125 46.0664 7.25195C46.2529 6.523 46.2715 5.78832 46.2715 4.95605V4.71387H47.4658V12.9697L46.5205 12.9932L46.2715 12.999V12.751C46.2715 11.9967 46.2362 11.2869 46.0986 10.5645C46.0269 10.2172 45.8757 9.93882 45.6689 9.75C45.4635 9.56261 45.1899 9.4502 44.8486 9.4502H41.3896V16.6182H46.8506C47.4505 16.6182 47.9768 16.5033 48.3945 16.2627C48.8075 16.0247 49.1284 15.6551 49.3135 15.1162C49.5854 14.3171 49.6556 13.5314 49.7266 12.5908L49.7432 12.3662H50.9199V17.8828H35.9795V16.7168L36.2109 16.707L38.5615 16.6006C38.7905 16.5853 38.9263 16.5248 39.0176 16.4043C39.1198 16.269 39.2031 16.0104 39.2031 15.5137V2.63574C39.2031 2.12357 39.141 1.78271 39.0303 1.57324C38.9773 1.4732 38.9148 1.40776 38.8457 1.36426C38.7771 1.32112 38.6887 1.2907 38.5693 1.2832L35.9795 1.18652V0.00195312H50.2471V5.37598Z" fill="currentColor"/>
</svg></div></a><nav role="navigation" class="nav_menu w-nav-menu"><a data-wf-native-id-path="973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050:0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de36d" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de36d" data-wf-component-context="%5B%7B%22componentId%22%3A%220387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de361%22%2C%22instanceId%22%3A%22973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050%22%7D%5D" href="/about" class="nav_menu_link w-nav-link">About</a><a data-wf-native-id-path="973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050:0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de36f" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de36f" data-wf-component-context="%5B%7B%22componentId%22%3A%220387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de361%22%2C%22instanceId%22%3A%22973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050%22%7D%5D" href="/jobs" class="nav_menu_link w-nav-link">Careers</a><a data-wf-native-id-path="973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050:0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de371" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de371" data-wf-component-context="%5B%7B%22componentId%22%3A%220387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de361%22%2C%22instanceId%22%3A%22973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050%22%7D%5D" href="/research-and-news" class="nav_menu_link w-nav-link">Research &amp; News</a><a data-wf-native-id-path="973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050:0b842c18-e57a-4110-2aa6-c70e3489bf08" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="0b842c18-e57a-4110-2aa6-c70e3489bf08" data-wf-component-context="%5B%7B%22componentId%22%3A%220387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de361%22%2C%22instanceId%22%3A%22973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050%22%7D%5D" href="/manifesto" class="nav_menu_link is-last w-nav-link">Manifest</a><a data-wf-native-id-path="973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050:0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de373" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de373" data-wf-component-context="%5B%7B%22componentId%22%3A%220387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de361%22%2C%22instanceId%22%3A%22973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050%22%7D%5D" href="#" class="button-mb is-secondary hide-dt open-contact w-button">Contact Us</a></nav><div class="nav_button w-nav-button"><div data-is-ix2-target="1" class="nav_menu-icon" data-w-id="d8334049-a353-5487-0a44-6699c1a44673" data-animation-type="lottie" data-src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/69a974f0b1fffb03644ecf2c/69b3104999d3f1683e90a14b_hamburger.json" data-loop="0" data-direction="1" data-autoplay="0" data-renderer="svg" data-default-duration="0" data-duration="1" data-loading="eager"></div></div><a data-wf-native-id-path="973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050:0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de377" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="0387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de377" data-wf-component-context="%5B%7B%22componentId%22%3A%220387f904-b277-ce93-0cec-578cb15de361%22%2C%22instanceId%22%3A%22973244b9-ce82-4f6a-edf8-02184c0d4050%22%7D%5D" href="#" class="nav-button hide-tablet open-contact w-button">Contact Us</a></div></div></div><main class="main-wrapper"><section class="small-hero-section"><div class="padding-global padding-section-medium is-small-bottom"><div class="container-small"><div class="blog-hero_component"><div class="blog_date">October 26, 2026</div><h1>Chained PHP2Shell Exploit Roots 25 Servers, including US Government Servers</h1><div class="text-weight-medium">Dream Research</div></div></div></div></section><section class="blog-post-section"><div class="padding-global padding-section-medium is-small-top"><div class="container-small"><div class="blog_rt w-richtext"><p><em>Updated: October 29, 2026.</em></p><p><em>Since this discovery, the Dream research team has been sharing updates and collaborating on an investigation with Palo Alto Networks, the vendor of the firewall in question. More information is available <a href="https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/cve-2026-109618-cve-2026-109619/">on the Palo Alto Networks website</a>. Additional details are now included in the post itself.</em></p><p><em>Custom PHP2Shell Exploit Chained with Kernel LPE to Root Versions of Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS Firewalls in the Wild</em></p><p>On October 25, Dream researchers uncovered artifacts of what appeared to be a Chinese AI-assisted campaign that rooted 25 servers, including systems in a US government-affiliated environment. The attacker combined PHP2Shell with a kernel privilege-escalation exploit to compromise exposed systems.</p><p>The affected devices run specific versions of Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS.</p><p>The exploited surface is the PAN-OS management web server, which also fronts the GlobalProtect portal. Palo Alto Networks’ Unit 42 has independently verified the attacker’s tooling and results, and Palo Alto Networks is releasing fixed PAN-OS versions across the affected branches.</p><p>After rooting the firewalls, the attacker launched attacks against Active Directory. The recovered artifacts confirm credential extraction and directory enumeration.</p><p>The operation illustrates how an exposed management interface can become a path into an organization’s identity infrastructure. For government defenders, vulnerability discovery is just the beginning, after which the real challenge extends from identifying vulnerable systems to understanding and containing the access a compromise makes possible.</p><p><em>The research below was conducted with proactive threat hunting using Dream’s autonomous cyber research product, <a href="https://dreamgroup.com/blog/hercules-on-premises-ai-vulnerability-model-ranked-1-on-cybergym">Hero</a>.</em></p><h2>Discovery</h2><p>Dream researchers identified a complete attack operation that included a custom weaponization of the PHP2Shell exploit chain (CVE-2026-109618/CVE-2026-109619) paired with the Fragnesia kernel privilege escalation (CVE-2026-46300), target lists, exploitation logs, and approximately 40 custom post-exploitation scripts.</p><p>This included artifacts of a campaign that had rooted 25 PAN-OS firewall and management servers across the United States, Japan, Taiwan, and South Korea. Once rooted, the attacker zeroed in on three specific servers in a US government-affiliated environment.</p><p>The discovered artifacts revealed an operation that began with exploit development in a lab environment and ended with persistent access to a US government organization's internal Active Directory domain.</p><h3>AI Usage in Attack</h3><p>We also observed some evidence of AI-assisted tooling in the attack infrastructure:</p><ul><li>Claude Code had been installed</li><li>An AI-powered offensive security platform called aiscan was configured with Chinese LLMs DeepSeek and Kimi</li><li>API keys for the Chinese reconnaissance platform FOFA were present</li></ul><p>While we have no direct evidence that the AI tools were specifically used to write the exploit or the post-exploitation scripts, the code structure, commenting style, and consistency across ~40 scripts suggest AI-assisted development. That said, this operation does not appear to be a <a href="https://dreamgroup.com/blog/inside-a-multi-agent-ai-framework-used-to-compromise-government-entities-in-asia">fully autonomous nation-state attack</a>.</p><h2>The Exploit: Two Vulnerabilities Chained Into One Request</h2><p>The attacker developed two separate exploits and chained them together to achieve root access on any internet-facing PAN-OS firewall running a vulnerable build in a single HTTP request without any credentials.</p><h3>PHP2Shell (CVE-2026-109618 / CVE-2026-109619) — Remote Code Execution</h3><p>The implementation we recovered is a custom, non-public build, not a copy of any known public exploit for PHP2Shell. It follows the same underlying concept but appears to have been independently developed and tailored specifically for PAN-OS.</p><p>The vulnerability targets PHP's <code>session.upload_progress</code> mechanism on the PAN-OS management web server (appweb). PAN-OS uses PHP 8.0.30 with a high memory limit that enables the memory corruption stages of the attack.</p><p>The attack works in four orchestrated stages:</p><ul><li><strong>A1 — Memory Exhaustion (~383MB):</strong> The attacker floods the server with crafted multipart POST requests, each containing massive filenames and session progress data. This pushes PHP's memory allocator to its limits, forcing the system into a predictable memory layout.</li><li><strong>B1 — Information Leak:</strong> A second request forges the stale session ID length from A1, forcing an out-of-bounds read. The server's <code>Set-Cookie</code> response header carries the leaked pointer value — giving the attacker the base address of <code>libphp.so</code> in the running process.</li><li><strong>A2 — Stale Reference Setup (~500MB):</strong> Another round of memory pressure initializes a session, leaving <code>http_session_vars</code> as a live <code>zend_reference</code>, then triggers a second OOM that leaves <code>mod_data=NULL</code> — creating a stale reference the attacker can forge in B2.</li><li><strong>B2 — Code Execution:</strong> The final request forges a PHP HashTable entry in the freed memory region. The forged entry's <code>pDestructor</code> callback points to <code>zif_system</code> (PHP's <code>system()</code> function), and its data points to a shell command string. When PHP's cleanup handler walks the HashTable, it calls the forged destructor — executing arbitrary commands as the <code>nobody</code> user.</li></ul><h3>Fragnesia (CVE-2026-46300) — Kernel Privilege Escalation</h3><p>Getting code execution as <code>nobody</code> isn't enough since SELinux confines <code>httpd_t</code> from modifying system files. In order to complete the root, the attacker chained in a second exploit: a Linux kernel vulnerability in the XFRM ESP-in-TCP subsystem.</p><p>Fragnesia uses page-cache writes to overwrite <code>/etc/pam.d/su</code> — replacing its authentication rules with <code>pam_permit.so</code> (accept all). Because the PAM file is only 774 bytes, the exploit uses a per-byte splicing variant to write it precisely. After the overwrite, <code>su root</code> succeeds without a password.</p><p>Both exploits run in a single B2 HTTP request. The total chain — from unauthenticated HTTPS connection to persistent root webshell — completes in under 15 minutes.</p><h2>The Lab: Version Profiling at Scale</h2><p>The exploit is not a single fire-and-forget payload. It requires precise memory offsets that differ between PAN-OS versions — the address of <code>zval_ptr_dtor</code>, the address of <code>zif_system</code>, HashTable structure sizes, and memory geometry parameters that control the heap layout during each stage.</p><p>Among the recovered artifacts, we found 11 JSON version profile files — one for each PAN-OS version that had been tested:</p><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div class='cve-table-wrapper'>
  <table class='cve-table'>
  <thead>
    <tr class='cve-table__row cve-table__row--head'><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>PAN-OS branch</th><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>PHP</th><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>Profiles</th><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>11.1</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>7.4.33</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>2 provisional</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>PHP 7.4 — RCE as nobody verified in lab, but full chain not calibrated (base build plus one hotfix variant)</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>12.1, 12.2</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>8.0.30</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>1 calibrated, 8 provisional</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>One base profile fully lab-tested; the others inherit its geometry or share offsets with neighboring builds</td></tr>  </tbody>
  </table>
</div></div><p>Each profile contains the exact offsets extracted from the <code>libphp.so</code> binary of that version — with SHA256 hashes of each library, ELF build IDs, and precise memory geometry parameters (chunk sizes, fill counts, drain counts, spray offsets) tuned through lab testing.</p><p>The difference between "calibrated" and "provisional" profiles is notable — calibrated means the attacker tested the exploit against a running VM of that version and confirmed the memory geometry works. Provisional means they calculated offsets from the binary but haven't verified on a live instance.</p><h2>The Campaign Funnel: From 19,000 Discovered to 25 Exploited</h2><figure class="w-richtext-figure-type-image w-richtext-align-fullwidth" style="max-width:600px" data-rt-type="image" data-rt-align="fullwidth" data-rt-max-width="600px"><div><img alt="Operational timeline in ten steps across setup, exploitation, pivot and post-exploitation, from October 8 to October 24." src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6a146a5aca0b1a41051708a5/6ae0916527e477c7abbb7f44_6ae090fd9d4636df82361954_PHP2Shell-fig-01-timeline-final.jpeg" width="auto" height="auto" loading="auto"></div><figcaption><strong><em>Figure 1: Operational timeline of the campaign, from setup on October 8 through exploitation, the pivot into the internal network, and post-exploitation on October 24.</em></strong></figcaption></figure><p>With the exploit ready and profiles for 9 exploitable versions, the attacker launched a targeting campaign that followed a clear funnel:</p><h3>Stage 1: Global Discovery (19,000 targets)</h3><p>Using FOFA — a Chinese internet asset search engine similar to Shodan — the attacker identified approximately 19,000 internet-facing PAN-OS firewalls worldwide. These were devices with management interfaces exposed to the internet, discoverable through certificate fingerprints and HTTP response signatures.</p><h3>Stage 2: Version Fingerprinting (5,000 alive)</h3><p>A custom version scanner ran with 200 concurrent threads against all 19,000 targets, identifying which devices were actually online and what PAN-OS version they were running. Approximately 5,000 responded with version information.</p><h3>Stage 3: Geographic and Version Filtering (240 targets)</h3><p>From the 5,000 alive devices, the attacker filtered to servers located in only United States, Japan, Taiwan, and South Korea. They then further filtered to versions matching their exploit profiles (the PAN-OS 12.1 and 12.2 branches running PHP 8.0.30). This produced a final target list of 240 firewalls.</p><p>Since US, Japan, Taiwan, and South Korea are the four primary strategic adversaries and intelligence targets for Chinese state-aligned cyber operations in the Pacific theater, this indicates potential nation-state adversary efforts.</p><h3>Stage 4: Automated Exploitation (24 rooted)</h3><p>The 240 targets were fed into an automated batch runner that exploited them five at a time, with an 1800-second (30-minute) timeout per target. For each success, the batch:</p><ul><li>Ran the full PHP2Shell + Fragnesia chain</li><li>Planted a 103-byte PHP root webshell in a publicly accessible directory on the firewall (<code>/unauth/php/change_password.php</code>)</li><li>Verified root by executing <code>id</code> through the webshell</li><li>Logged the target's hostname and IP to a success CSV</li><li>Moved to the next target</li></ul><p>At this point, 24 firewalls had been successfully rooted.</p><h3>Stage 5: Triage (2 firewalls prioritized)</h3><p>For 22 of the 24 rooted firewalls, the attacker did nothing beyond planting the webshell. No persistence, no tunnels, no post-exploitation tools. The servers were effectively <strong>owned and parked</strong>, allowing the attacker to return through the webshell at any time.</p><p>Two servers seemingly associated with one target progressed beyond this stage. Both appeared to belong to a US government-affiliated environment.</p><h3>Stage 6: The Pivot (Shifting from Firewall to Domain)</h3><p>Among the 24 rooted firewalls, two belonged to a US government-affiliated organization. The running configuration extracted from the first firewall revealed an environment with characteristics suggesting government affiliation: CAC (Common Access Card) authentication, a specific naming prefix, and standardized government-style asset naming. The configuration file contained the organization's complete internal network topology: domain controllers, a central firewall management server, a Keycloak SSO server, a GitLab instance, GlobalProtect VPN gateways, and the Active Directory domain.</p><p>The first firewall was rooted on Oct 23 at 18:14 UTC during the automated batch. But it crashed shortly after — likely from the ~900MB of exploit traffic — and never came back online. The attacker monitored it with 240 automated probes over two hours, waiting for it to recover. It didn't. The second firewall, whose certificate data definitively linked it to the target organization, had timed out during the original batch.</p><p>Recognizing the organization's value from the first firewall's configuration, the attacker created a separate, dedicated campaign and successfully exploited it on Oct 24 at 10:09 UTC.</p><p>Within hours, the attacker:</p><p><strong>Built a persistent tunnel into the internal network.</strong></p><p>An 8-port reverse SSH tunnel connected the compromised firewall back to the operator's infrastructure, forwarding access to:</p><ul><li>Domain controllers (LDAP, LDAPS, RDP)</li><li>The internal firewall management server</li><li>GlobalProtect VPN infrastructure</li><li>A SOCKS5 proxy for arbitrary internal network access</li></ul><p>The tunnel was wrapped in a systemd service and an <code>rc.local</code> boot hook, both launching scripts from <code>/usr/local/lib/.panops/</code> — it survives reboots and automatically reconnects.</p><p><strong>Exploited the management server through the tunnel.</strong> At 11:35 UTC, less than 90 minutes after rooting the second firewall, the attacker used the newly gained server access and the same PHP2Shell + Fragnesia chain against a third government server - the internal firewall management server that was only reachable through the tunnel. This server centrally manages all of the organization's firewalls. Compromising it gave the attacker access to every managed firewall's configuration, credentials, and network settings. This brought the total number of servers exploited to 25.</p><p><strong>Launched an AD attack campaign from the management server.</strong> Because the management server already had LDAP connectivity to Active Directory (it needs it to authenticate VPN users), the attacker used it as their launch platform for domain attacks:</p><ul><li>Decrypted the LDAP service account password — the device config stores it encrypted, but with root access the attacker has the master key. They extracted it in plaintext and hardcoded it into every subsequent script.</li><li>Enumerated every user and group in the domain via LDAP queries.</li><li>Sprayed passwords against every user account using organization-themed passwords. The domain had <code>lockoutThreshold=0</code> — meaning no account lockout, allowing unlimited spray attempts.</li><li>Attempted to create backdoor AD accounts — both a user account (tried in every OU) and a machine account (exploiting the default <code>MachineAccountQuota</code> setting).</li><li>Analyzed AD ACL chains for privilege escalation paths.</li><li>Attempted SAML signing key extraction from the Keycloak IdP configuration.</li><li>Probed Keycloak SSO for authentication bypass.</li></ul><p>The attack results — whether the password spray found valid credentials, whether the backdoor accounts were created, whether SAML keys were extracted — stayed on the management server's local filesystem and were not among the artifacts we recovered. This means we cannot determine the full extent of the compromise.</p><figure class="w-richtext-figure-type-image w-richtext-align-fullwidth" style="max-width:1852px" data-rt-type="image" data-rt-align="fullwidth" data-rt-max-width="1852px"><div><img alt="Attack path from the attacker across the internet to a firewall at the perimeter, through a tunnel to the firewall management server, and on to the domain controllers." src="https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/6a146a5aca0b1a41051708a5/6adf8c6612cf8377560226a2_6adf87f5ffee46727b95f733_PHP2Shell-fig-02-attack-path-neutral.png" width="auto" height="auto" loading="auto"></div><figcaption><strong><em>Figure 2: The attack path. PHP2Shell and Fragnesia root the perimeter firewall, a reverse SSH tunnel reaches the internal management server, and the decrypted LDAP service account opens Active Directory.</em></strong></figcaption></figure><h3>Management Server → Active Directory</h3><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div class='cve-table-wrapper'>
  <table class='cve-table'>
  <thead>
    <tr class='cve-table__row cve-table__row--head'><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>Status</th><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>Activity</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>✓</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>LDAP password decrypted from config</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>✓</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>Full user &amp; group enumeration</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>?</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>Password spray (all users, no lockout)</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>?</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>Backdoor user + machine account creation</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>?</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>SAML signing key extraction</td></tr>  </tbody>
  </table>
</div></div><h2>What the Attacker Has Now</h2><p>From the recovered artifacts, we can confirm the attacker possesses:</p><ul><li>Persistent remote access to the organization's internal network via a boot-surviving reverse tunnel and SOCKS proxy — likely still active</li><li>Root webshells on two firewalls and the firewall management server — likely still active</li><li>The full Active Directory user and group listing — a complete organizational blueprint</li><li>Decrypted LDAP service account credentials — enabling direct AD queries at any time</li><li>Private keys harvested from the PAN-OS firewall filesystem — HA replication keys, SSL/TLS certificate keys, and device authentication keys</li><li>Complete network topology — every IP, hostname, subnet, server role, and VPN gateway</li><li>SAML IdP configuration with weak security settings (<code>validate-idp-certificate: no</code>)</li><li>22 additional parked firewalls across the US, Japan, Taiwan, and South Korea — ready for exploitation at any time</li></ul><p>What we cannot determine is whether the password spray, backdoor account creation, or SAML key extraction succeeded. If any of these attacks worked, the compromise extends far beyond the perimeter firewalls into the organization's identity infrastructure.</p><h2>Attribution Indicators</h2><p>We do not attribute this activity to a named threat actor. Palo Alto Networks’ Unit 42 tracks this activity as CL-UNK-1280 and attributes it to a Chinese-speaking attacker in the UTC+8 time zone. However, multiple indicators point to a Chinese-nexus operation:</p><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div class='cve-table-wrapper'>
  <table class='cve-table'>
  <thead>
    <tr class='cve-table__row cve-table__row--head'><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>Indicator</th><th class='cve-table__cell cve-table__cell--head'>Detail</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>Target selection</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>US, Japan, Taiwan, South Korea exclusively. These are the primary strategic intelligence targets for Chinese state-aligned operations in the Pacific.</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>FOFA</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>The internet reconnaissance platform used to discover targets is a Chinese service.</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>iox</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>A Chinese-developed tunneling utility observed among the recovered tools.</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>DeepSeek and Kimi</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>The LLMs configured in the aiscan offensive security platform are Chinese language models.</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>UTC+8 timestamps</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>The infrastructure's clock ran on China Standard Time, confirmed by comparing log timestamps against HTTP Date: response headers from targets.</td></tr>    <tr class='cve-table__row'><td class='cve-table__cell'>Chinese-language prompts</td><td class='cve-table__cell'>The aiscan session logs contain user messages in Chinese.</td></tr>  </tbody>
  </table>
</div></div><h2>Conclusion</h2><p>This operation demonstrates a level of preparation and capability that sets it apart from opportunistic exploitation. The attacker invested significant effort into vulnerability research — obtaining PAN-OS firmware, decrypting it, extracting PHP libraries, building precise memory profiles for 9 different software versions, and calibrating exploit geometry through lab testing. The exploitation itself was automated and efficient: 240 targets attempted in a single batch, with version-specific profiles selected automatically.</p><p>There was a clear-cut focus on government targets.</p><p>The attacker scanned 19,000 firewalls, rooted 24, and immediately identified the two that mattered. Within hours of compromising a US government-affiliated firewall, they had tunneled into the internal network, compromised a third server using the same PHP2Shell exploit, and launched a full Active Directory attack campaign — all through a chain that started with a single unauthenticated HTTPS request to a management interface that should never have been exposed to the internet.</p><p>The 22 other rooted firewalls remain parked with active webshells. The persistent tunnel into the US government organization is likely still running. And the attacker still has 19,000 fingerprinted targets they can revisit as they develop profiles for additional PAN-OS versions. Palo Alto Networks device telemetry shows continued exploitation attempts against exposed PAN-OS devices.</p><p>See the <a href="https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/cve-2026-109618-cve-2026-109619/">Palo Alto Networks website</a> for full guidance and indicators of compromise.</p><h2>Closing the Gap Between Discovery and Defense</h2><p>For governments and critical infrastructure, finding a vulnerability is only the beginning. Defenders need to identify exposed systems, establish what attackers can exploit, and act before a foothold becomes a wider compromise.</p><p>Dream addresses that challenge through two complementary cyberdefense platforms:</p><ul><li><strong>Sphere scales national cyber defense.</strong> It maps exposure, prioritizes risks, and detects and responds to attacks across government networks, tailored to each nation’s threats and infrastructure.</li><li><strong>Hero scales cyber research.</strong> Its autonomous research agents help experts investigate threats, discover and validate vulnerabilities—including in software without source code—and develop remediation guidance and patches. This is the tool that was used by Dream researchers to conduct the investigation detailed in this post.</li></ul><p>Together, they connect deeper investigation with defense across the national attack surface.</p></div><div class="blog_cta"><h2 class="blog_cta-heading">Get Dream&#x27;s research and briefings first</h2><div class="contact_form-block is-news w-form"><form id="wf-form-Subscribe-Form" name="wf-form-Subscribe-Form" data-name="Subscribe Form" method="get" class="news_form" data-wf-page-id="6a146a5b836a79f52b7fa7e2" data-wf-element-id="908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc2587e"><div class="form_field-wrapper is-news"><label for="newsEmail" class="sr-only">Email</label><input class="form_input is-news w-input" maxlength="256" name="Email" data-name="Email" placeholder="Email" type="email" id="newsEmail" required=""/></div><input type="submit" data-wait="Please wait..." id="w-node-_908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc25883-2b7fa7e2" data-wf-native-id-path="908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc25883" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc25883" class="button is-gold w-button" value="Submit"/><label id="w-node-_908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc25884-2b7fa7e2" data-wf-native-id-path="908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc25884" data-wf-ao-click-engagement-tracking="true" data-wf-element-id="908da7ca-8088-4c75-60d3-363f5cc25884" class="w-checkbox form_checkbox"><input type="checkbox" id="checkbox" name="checkbox" data-name="Check